<%
  running = %w[pending running].include?(@validation.state)
  m = @validation.manifest
  done = m.is_a?(Hash) && @validation.state == "done"
  summary = done ? (m["summary"] || {}) : {}
  stats = done ? (m["stats"] || {}) : {}
  static = done ? (stats["static"] || {}) : {}
  # The training set the run asked for (validation.variants). ABSENT is the
  # default: the opt-in is off unless the spec said so, and a proof that did not
  # ask for one must render exactly as it did before this existed -- so this is
  # nil, and the card below is not emitted at all, rather than emitted empty or
  # with a "did not run" note.
  dataset = done && m["dataset"].is_a?(Hash) ? m["dataset"] : nil
%>

<% if RECORD_TEMPLATE_V2 %>
  <%#
    THE RECORD HEAD, from the shared skeleton (facelift 2/4, issue #94):
    breadcrumb -> gradient heading -> badge/meta row -> alias line.

    A proof is a CANDIDATE's record, so the trail runs Projects / <job> / this
    proof, and the proof's own display name is the crumb's terminal node in mono.
    The state is a badge (the same `.badge.<state>` vocabulary a job's state
    uses), and the mono line under it carries the two files this record is made
    of -- the candidate it proved and the render it produced. The prose that was
    the page's sub-title is the alias line, where the skeleton puts a record's
    second name.
  %>
  <%= render "application/record_header",
        crumbs: [["Projects", projects_path], [@job.name, job_path(@job)]],
        crumb_id: proof_name(@validation.file),
        title: "Proof #{proof_label(@validation.file)}",
        badges: content_tag(:span, @validation.state, class: "badge #{@validation.state}"),
        meta: [@validation.file, (done ? m["proof"] : nil)].compact.join(" · "),
        alias_line: "Layer A (source checks) + Layer B (render preflight)." \
                    "#{running ? ' Give it a moment; this refreshes itself.' : ''}" %>
<% end %>

<div class="page-head">
  <%#
    Both controls sit on the Proof line rather than off to the side
    or at the foot of the page. Ask Jev is a decorated button because it is
    the action you take; the manifest is a text block because it is a thing
    you read, and it matches the findings entries below rather than competing
    with them. Both still open a large popup. The title's margin-right:auto
    keeps them pushed to the right edge of the line.
  %>
  <div class="page-head-row page-head-row--split page-head-row--stacked">
    <%# THE PREVIOUS PER-PAGE HEAD, kept behind the skeleton (issue #94, story 5).
        The title (grid row 1 col 1) and the blurb (row 2 col 1) are the two parts
        the skeleton now renders; `RECORD_TEMPLATE_V2 = false` in
        config/initializers/record_template.rb brings both back and this head is
        exactly what it was. Only ONE branch renders, so the page never carries
        two <h1>s, and the two controls below are unchanged in either branch --
        which is why they keep the box and the grid slots they were measured at. %>
    <% unless RECORD_TEMPLATE_V2 %>
    <h1 class="section-title">Proof <span class="gradient-text"><%= proof_label(@validation.file) %></span></h1>
    <% end %>

    <button type="button"
            class="btn btn-primary btn-sm jev-btn head-action head-action--top"
            title="Ask Jev about this proof"
            data-popup="jev-popup"
            data-jev-url="<%= ask_jev_validation_path(@validation, format: :json) %>"
            data-jev-csrf="<%= form_authenticity_token %>"
            data-has-answers="<%= @validation.jev_answers.present? ? 1 : 0 %>"
            <%= "disabled" if running %>>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="jev-btn-icon">
        <path d="M4 19.5V6.75A1.75 1.75 0 0 1 5.75 5h8.5L19 9.75v9.75H5.75A1.75 1.75 0 0 1 4 19.5z" />
        <path d="M13.5 5.5v4.75H18" class="orb-fold" />
        <path d="m7.75 15.5 2.4-2.6 1.9 1.7 3-3.3" class="orb-rule" />
      </svg>
      <span>Ask Jev</span>
    </button>

    <button type="button"
            class="manifest-block head-action head-action--below"
            title="Manifest (JSON)"
            data-popup="manifest-popup">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="manifest-block-icon">
        <path d="M6 2.75h8.5L20 8.25V21.25H6z" />
        <path d="M14 2.75V8.5h6" class="orb-fold" />
        <path d="M9 12.5h7M9 16h7" class="orb-rule" />
      </svg>
      <span class="manifest-block-text">manifest</span>
    </button>

    <%# THE TWO GATES (#44). The manifest block explains what the run RECORDED;
        this explains what the run DID -- the two checks a finding's layer
        refers to, and why they can disagree. Both are text blocks, so they sit
        together, and both open a popup through the app's ONE mechanism. %>
    <button type="button"
            class="manifest-block head-action head-action--below"
            title="The two gates — what each layer checks"
            data-popup="proof-gates-popup">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="manifest-block-icon">
        <path d="M6 2.75h8.5L20 8.25V21.25H6z" />
        <path d="M14 2.75V8.5h6" class="orb-fold" />
        <path d="M9 12.5h7M9 16h7" class="orb-rule" />
      </svg>
      <span class="manifest-block-text">gates</span>
    </button>

    <%# The blurb lives INSIDE the row, in grid row 2 col 1, so it reads as the
        title's sub-line. In .page-sub it was a sibling of the row and rendered
        below the whole block, sharing a line with the actions.
        KEPT BEHIND THE SKELETON: this is the alias line now, and it renders
        here only when RECORD_TEMPLATE_V2 is off. %>
    <% unless RECORD_TEMPLATE_V2 %>
    <p class="page-head-blurb">
      <span class="mono"><%= proof_name(@validation.file) %></span> — Layer A (source checks) + Layer B (render preflight).
      <% if running %>Give it a moment; this refreshes itself.<% end %>
    </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <div class="page-sub">
    <div class="page-sub-main">
      <div class="actions-row">
        <%= link_to "↻ Refresh", refresh_validation_path(@validation), class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
        <%# A bare back arrow with the destination in the tooltip rather than the
            job's name as visible text: that name is already the heading, so
            naming it on the button repeated the title. A real <a>, so it is
            keyboard- and middle-clickable. %>
        <%= link_to "<--", job_path(@job), class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm",
                    title: "Back to Job", aria: { label: "Back to Job" } %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <% if done %>
    <div class="job-stats" aria-label="Run summary">
      <div class="job-stat"><div class="value" style="color:<%= summary["hard"].to_i > 0 ? 'var(--danger)' : 'var(--success)' %>"><%= summary["hard"] %></div><div class="label">hard</div></div>
      <div class="job-stat"><div class="value" style="color:var(--warning)"><%= summary["advisory"] %></div><div class="label">advisory</div></div>
      <div class="job-stat"><div class="value"><%= stats["rendered_ink_colors"] || "–" %></div><div class="label">inks</div></div>
      <div class="job-stat"><div class="value"><%= static["path_nodes_total"] || "–" %></div><div class="label">nodes</div></div>
    </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</div>

<% if @validation.error.present? %>
  <div class="flash alert mono"><%= @validation.error %></div>
<% end %>

<% if running %>
  <div class="card card-glow" data-poll-url="<%= validation_path(@validation, format: :json) %>" data-poll-interval="1500">
    <h3><span class="spinner"></span>&nbsp; Proof is running…</h3>
    <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);margin:0.5rem 0 1rem;">validate_svg.py → preflight.py → proof + print PDF + manifest.</p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<% if done %>
  <div class="grid-2">
    <div class="card">
      <%= record_section("Proof render") %>
      <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
        Inkscape render at <%= stats["dpi"] %> DPI — the visual truth the metrics can't replace.
      </p>
      <%# The render opens in the glass preview popup, like every other image
          in the studio, so the proof can be favourited, compared against the
          source/inverse and colour-shifted without leaving the page. The href
          is the JS-off fallback. %>
      <a class="art-frame" href="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: m["proof"]) %>"
         data-cs-view="svg" data-cs-url="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: m["proof"]) %>"
         data-cs-title="<%= m["proof"] %>"
         data-cs-job="<%= @job.id %>" data-cs-name="<%= m["proof"] %>"
         data-cs-ref-source="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: "source.png") %>"
         data-cs-ref-inverse="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: "source.inverse.png") %>"
         title="Proof render — click to view">
        <img src="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: m["proof"]) %>" alt="Proof render">
      </a>
      <div class="actions-row">
        <%= link_to "⬇ Proof PNG", file_job_path(@job, name: m["proof"]), class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
        <%= link_to "⬇ Print PDF", file_job_path(@job, name: m["print_pdf"]), class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card">
      <%= record_section("Findings") %>
      <%
        # The manifest records only what went WRONG, so a proof that cleared every
        # gate used to render as an empty card -- which reads as "nothing was
        # checked". When no hard gate fired, say what it cleared, positively, with
        # the numbers that carry it. See cleared_checks() in ApplicationHelper.
        findings = Array(m["findings"])
        hard_count = findings.count { |f| f["severity"] == "hard" }
        # The run's OWN spec rides along: two Layer A gates can be disarmed by
        # it, and a disarmed gate produces no finding either -- see
        # cleared_checks() in ApplicationHelper.
        clearances = hard_count.zero? ? cleared_checks(m, spec: @job&.spec) : { cleared: [], skipped: [] }
      %>
      <%# A FINDING, AND WHAT TO CHANGE ABOUT IT (#44).
          A finding used to name a rule and a detail, and nothing said what to
          change. The mapping existed only in svg/docs/HOWTO-print-check.md and in
          a Jev-keyed table, so it lived nowhere an operator could reach from the
          page. ProofRemedies carries it by RULE ID, and this row renders it.

          HARD and ADVISORY are SEPARATE groups, the way the manifest already
          separates them (preflight.py classify_findings): an advisory is not a
          lesser hard finding, and one undifferentiated list is what let an
          advisory read as a failure.

          A rule with no entry renders the pill, the layer and the detail and
          NOTHING ELSE -- no empty meaning, no empty suggestion. That is the
          revert: remove the suggestion column and every un-tabled rule is
          byte-for-byte what it was. %>
      <%
        findings_by_severity = findings.group_by { |f| f["severity"] == "hard" ? :hard : :advisory }
      %>
      <% %i[hard advisory].each do |severity| %>
        <% group = findings_by_severity[severity].to_a %>
        <% next if group.empty? %>
        <div class="proof-findings proof-findings--<%= severity %>">
          <p class="proof-findings-head"><%= severity == :hard ? "Hard findings — the file cannot be printed as specified" : "Advisory — reported, does not fail the run" %></p>
          <% group.each do |f| %>
            <% remedy = ProofRemedies.entry_for(f["rule"]) %>
            <div class="proof-finding">
              <span class="status-pill proof-finding-rule status-<%= f["severity"] == "hard" ? "fail" : "error" %>"><%= f["rule"] %></span>
              <span style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:0.85rem;"> layer <%= f["layer"] %></span>
              <%# THE EPISTEMIC FLAG (#9): this proof's claims are unverified.
                  Rendered on the FINDING's own row, because the finding IS the
                  claim -- the chip belongs to the row it marks, and the row is
                  where the operator reads the rule. Nothing renders for an
                  accepted proof, which is every row today. %>
              <%= render "shared/epistemic_chip", flags: @validation.epistemic_flags %>
              <div style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:0.85rem;margin-top:0.3rem;"><%= f["detail"] %></div>
              <%# The two new columns. Rendered ONLY for a rule the table carries,
                  so an un-tabled rule keeps exactly the markup it had. %>
              <% if remedy %>
                <div class="proof-finding-meaning"><%= remedy[:meaning] %></div>
                <% change = remedy[:change] %>
                <% if change %>
                  <div class="proof-finding-change">
                    <% case change[:kind]
                       when :spec_key %>
                      Change <span class="mono"><%= change[:key] %></span> — <%= change[:to] %>
                    <% when :script %>
                      Run <span class="mono"><%= change[:script] %></span> <%= change[:args] %>
                    <% when :template %>
                      Use the <span class="mono"><%= change[:template] %></span> template — <%= change[:label] %>
                    <% when :artwork, :route %>
                      <%= change[:action].to_s.capitalize %>
                    <% end %>
                    <% doc_url = ProofRemedies.doc_url_for(f["rule"]) %>
                    <% if doc_url %>
                      <a href="<%= doc_url %>"
                         class="proof-finding-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
                         title="The print check's own account of this rule">why this check</a>
                    <% end %>
                  </div>
                <% end %>
              <% end %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        </div>
      <% end %>

      <%# The clean pass keeps its own sentence, and it is suppressed the moment
          ANY finding exists -- including an advisory-only one, because an
          advisory is still a finding and the page must not claim a clean pass
          through a file that was reported on. %>
      <% if findings.empty? %>
        <p style="color:var(--success);margin-top:0.75rem;">No findings — clean pass through both layers.</p>
      <% end %>

      <% if clearances[:cleared].any? %>
        <p class="cleared-head">No hard findings — this proof clears:</p>
        <ul class="cleared-list">
          <% clearances[:cleared].each do |c| %>
            <li>
              <span class="cleared-label"><%= c[:label] %></span>
              <span class="cleared-area mono"><%= c[:area] %></span>
              <span class="cleared-detail"><%= c[:detail] %></span>
            </li>
          <% end %>
        </ul>
      <% end %>

      <% if clearances[:skipped].any? %>
        <p class="cleared-skipped">Not run (not cleared): <%= clearances[:skipped].to_sentence %>.</p>
      <% end %>
    </div>

    <% if dataset %>
      <%
        # THE TRAINING SET. Only a run that asked for it (spec.validation.variants)
        # has one, and what it holds is COPIES of the proof -- so the card says
        # that plainly rather than dressing them up as results. The pipeline did
        # not pick, rank or improve any of them; it derived them mechanically, and
        # they exist to be a dataset rather than a shortlist.
        #
        # Every file is read through the studio's own file proxy, so the card gets
        # the same popup and the same favourite behaviour as every other image.
        #
        # The paths are NOT all the same shape, and getting this wrong is how the
        # card shipped with a broken image: the runner writes the three FLAT
        # names -- `<stem>.dataset.json`, `<stem>.dataset.md` and
        # `<stem>.dataset-contact-sheet.png` -- at the JOB ROOT precisely so the
        # existing three-segment file route serves them (runner.py says so in its
        # own comment). Only the per-transform variants are one level deeper, in
        # `<stem>.dataset/raster|vector/<name>`, and ONLY those need the
        # four-segment form via `candidate:`.
        #
        # So the contact sheet and the dataset manifest below are requested by
        # name alone. Passing `candidate:` for them builds
        # `/files/<job>/<stem>.dataset/<stem>.dataset-contact-sheet.png`, which is
        # a 404 on a real runner -- and a broken <img> on the most prominent
        # element of this card.
        ds_candidate = @validation.file.to_s
        ds_sheet = dataset["sheet"].to_s
        ds_manifest = dataset["manifest"].to_s
        ds_transforms = Array(dataset["transforms"])
      %>
      <div class="card" data-proof-dataset>
        <%= record_section("Training set") %>
        <p style="color:var(--text-secondary);font-size:0.85rem;margin-bottom:1rem;">
          <%= dataset["count"].to_i %> similar copies of this proof,
          <%= ds_transforms.size %> transforms, derived by the print check.
        </p>

        <% if ds_sheet.present? %>
          <a class="art-frame"
             href="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: ds_sheet) %>"
             data-cs-view="svg"
             data-cs-url="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: ds_sheet) %>"
             data-cs-title="<%= ds_sheet %>"
             data-cs-job="<%= @job.id %>" data-cs-name="<%= ds_sheet %>"
             title="Training set contact sheet — click to view">
            <img src="<%= file_job_path(@job, name: ds_sheet) %>"
                 alt="Training set contact sheet">
          </a>
        <% end %>

        <div class="actions-row">
          <%= link_to "⬇ Training set 7z",
                dataset_job_path(@job, candidate: ds_candidate),
                class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
          <% if ds_manifest.present? %>
            <%= link_to "⬇ Dataset manifest",
                  file_job_path(@job, name: ds_manifest, download: 1),
                  class: "btn btn-secondary btn-sm" %>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <% if ds_transforms.any? %>
          <p class="substrate-screens">
            Transforms:
            <% ds_transforms.each do |t| %><%= content_tag(:span, t, class: "screen-chip") %><% end %>
          </p>
        <% end %>

        <p class="hint"><%= help_tip("proof.variants") %></p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%#
    The manifest moved out of the page body into the popup above. The inline
    <pre> is still emitted, hidden, so the orb has something to show without a
    second request -- and so it works with JS disabled.
  %>
</div>

<div class="popup" id="manifest-popup" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
     aria-labelledby="manifest-popup-title" hidden>
  <div class="popup-panel popup-wide">
    <header class="popup-head">
      <h2 id="manifest-popup-title">Manifest <span class="mono"><%= proof_name(@validation.file) %></span></h2>
      <button type="button" class="popup-close" data-popup-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    </header>
    <div class="popup-body">
      <% if m.is_a?(Hash) %>
        <% sub = m["substrate"] || {} %>
        <% if sub["is_fabric"] %>
          <%#
            The fabric colour is called out separately from the screens. It is
            in the palette and it is in the SVG, but nothing is printed for it:
            it is the garment showing through, so counting it as an ink would
            overstate the laydown.
          %>
          <div class="substrate-chip" style="--chip:<%= sub["colour"] %>">
            <span class="chip-swatch" aria-hidden="true"></span>
            <span class="chip-body">
              <strong><%= sub["colour"] %></strong> is the fabric
              <span class="chip-sub">not inked — no screen laid down for this colour</span>
            </span>
          </div>
          <p class="substrate-screens">
            Screens:
            <% Array(sub["screens"]).each do |c| %><%= content_tag(:span, c, class: "screen-chip") %><% end %>
          </p>
        <% end %>
        <div class="log-console popup-console"><pre><%= JSON.pretty_generate(m) %></pre></div>
      <% else %>
        <p class="popup-empty">No manifest yet — the proof has not finished.</p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="popup" id="jev-popup" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
     aria-labelledby="jev-popup-title" hidden>
  <div class="popup-panel">
    <header class="popup-head">
      <h2 id="jev-popup-title">Jev <span class="mono">· System One</span></h2>
      <button type="button" class="popup-close" data-popup-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    </header>
    <div class="popup-body" data-jev-body>
      <% if @validation.jev_answers.present? %>
        <% jev_partial = "validations/jev_answers" %>
        <%= render partial: jev_partial, locals: { validation: @validation } %>
      <% else %>
        <p class="popup-empty">
          Four typed advisory questions — dominant issue, next action, evidence
          quality, whether a human should eyeball the proof. Advisory only; the
          deterministic gates stay untouched.
        </p>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<%#
  THE TWO GATES (#44) — the popup that explains what a finding's layer means.

  The proof runs two checks, and the manifest records which layer produced each
  finding. The page states the counts; this states the MECHANISM, because
  "source_validation" and "render_preflight" are not self-describing and the
  whole point of the separation is that they can disagree:

    Layer A READS THE FILE. Fast, deterministic, source-only. It can refuse a
      file that would render perfectly well — a 0.85pt hairline is an ordinary
      line on screen, and only the source knows it will break up on press.
    Layer B RENDERS THE FILE and measures what it actually prints. Slower, and
      it sees what Layer A structurally cannot: a two-stop gradient is 2 colours
      in the file and hundreds once rasterised.

  WHERE THEY DISAGREE IS INFORMATIVE, and that is stated rather than smoothed
  over — the same sentence the print check's own doc carries. A gradient is
  refused by Layer A and counted by Layer B, and neither is wrong.

  Rendered through the app's ONE popup shape (.popup > .popup-panel > head +
  body) with `data-popup` on the trigger, so aether.js's delegated wirePopups
  opens and closes it exactly like every other popup. Nothing here reinvents a
  mechanism.
%>
<div class="popup" id="proof-gates-popup" role="dialog" aria-modal="true"
     aria-labelledby="proof-gates-popup-title" hidden>
  <div class="popup-panel popup-help">
    <header class="popup-head">
      <h2 id="proof-gates-popup-title">The two gates</h2>
      <button type="button" class="popup-close" data-popup-close aria-label="Close">&times;</button>
    </header>
    <div class="popup-body">
      <p class="help-tip-detail">
        A proof runs <strong>two</strong> checks, and a finding names the layer
        that produced it.
      </p>
      <dl class="help-notes">
        <dt>Layer A — <span class="mono">source_validation</span></dt>
        <dd>Reads the file. Fast and deterministic: raster embeds, declared
            colours, hairline strokes, open paths, zero-area shapes, node counts,
            the physical size, colours outside the palette.</dd>
        <dt>Layer B — <span class="mono">render_preflight</span></dt>
        <dd>Renders the file and measures what it prints: how many inks the
            artwork really needs, continuous tone, total ink coverage, placed
            bitmap resolution.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="help-tip-detail">
        <strong>Where they disagree is informative.</strong> A two-stop gradient
        is <span class="mono">2</span> colours in the file and <span class="mono">hundreds</span>
        once rendered: Layer B measures the hundreds, and Layer A refuses the file
        outright when the spec disallows gradients — so the ban holds even where
        no renderer was available. Neither layer is a corrected version of the
        other.
      </p>
      <p class="help-tip-change mono">
        svg/docs/HOWTO-print-check.md — "The two gates"
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>
<% end %>
